# minimum qore version
%requires qore >= 2.0
%requires json
%requires Mime
%requires Util
%requires Logger
%requires DataProvider
%requires HttpClientIo
%requires HttpClientStreamingIo
%requires(reexport) ConnectionProvider >= 1.4

%modern

module McpClient {
    version = "1.0";
    desc = "McpClient module for connecting to MCP servers as a client";
    author = "David Nichols <david@qore.org>";
    url = "http://qore.org";
    license = "MIT";
    init = sub () {
        ConnectionSchemeCache::registerScheme("mcp", McpConnection::ConnectionScheme);
        ConnectionSchemeCache::registerScheme("mcps", McpConnection::ConnectionScheme);
    };
}

/** @mainpage McpClient Module

    @tableofcontents

    @section mcpclientintro Introduction to the McpClient Module

    The %McpClient module provides a client implementation for the Model Context Protocol (MCP),
    allowing Qore applications to connect to MCP servers and interact with tools, resources,
    and prompts.

    The module supports all MCP protocol versions:
    - 2024-11-05 (initial version)
    - 2025-03-26 (March 2025)
    - 2025-06-18 (June 2025 - Enterprise readiness)
    - 2025-11-25 (November 2025 - Tasks, Extensions)
    - 2026-07-28 (July 2026 - stateless core, MRTR, subscriptions)

    @section McpClient_eras Protocol Eras

    MCP became a <b>stateless</b> protocol in revision
    @ref McpClient::McpVersion::V2026_07_28 "2026-07-28": there is no \c initialize handshake
    and no session, every request carries its own protocol version, client identity, and
    capabilities, and a server can no longer send requests of its own.

    This client speaks both shapes and works out which one the server uses on its own.
    @ref McpClient::McpClient::initialize() "initialize()" remains the single entry point: it
    attempts a stateless request first and falls back to the handshake if the server turns out
    to implement an earlier revision, caching the outcome for the life of the object.  Pin a
    protocol version with the \c protocol_version option to skip the probe.  Everything below
    the API — per-request metadata, header mirroring, version renegotiation, input round
    trips, and task polling — is handled transparently, so the calling code does not change
    with the protocol version in use.  Check which era is in force with
    @ref McpClient::McpClient::isModern() "isModern()".

    Methods that no longer exist in 2026-07-28 —
    @ref McpClient::McpClient::ping() "ping()" and
    @ref McpClient::McpClient::setLogLevel() "setLogLevel()" — throw \c MCP-DEPRECATED-METHOD
    against a modern server and name what to use instead; they continue to work against an
    earlier-revision server.

    @subsection McpClient_server_requests Answering the server

    A server may need an LLM completion, user input, or the client's root list before it can
    finish a request.  Register a callback for each kind of interaction you can service and
    the client answers on its own, whichever protocol revision is in force:

    @code{.py}
    client.setCallback("onElicitation", auto sub (hash<auto> params) {
        return {"action": "accept", "content": collectFromUser(params.requestedSchema)};
    });
    client.setCallback("onSampling", auto sub (hash<auto> params) {
        return askTheModel(params);
    });
    client.setCallback("onListRoots", auto sub (hash<auto> params) {
        return {"roots": ({"uri": "file:///workspace", "name": "workspace"},)};
    });
    @endcode

    Registering one of these callbacks also declares the matching client capability, so the
    server is never told this client supports something it cannot answer, and never asks for
    something it was not told about.  On 2026-07-28 the answers are attached to an automatic
    retry of the original request; on earlier revisions they are posted back as JSON-RPC
    responses to requests that arrive on the event stream, which must be open
    (@ref McpClient::McpClient::startSseListener() "startSseListener()").

    @subsection McpClient_features Features

    - Full MCP protocol support with automatic era detection and version negotiation
    - Async I/O via @ref HttpClientIo::HttpClientConnectionManager with automatic
      protocol negotiation (HTTP/3 → HTTP/2 → HTTP/1.1) and connection pooling
    - All three transports, selected automatically: Streamable HTTP, and the deprecated
      HTTP+SSE transport of protocol 2024-11-05, where the client opens an event stream with
      \c GET and is told by an \c endpoint event where to post
    - Server-to-client requests answered from callbacks in both eras: as Multi Round-Trip
      Request input on 2026-07-28, and as JSON-RPC requests on the event stream on earlier
      revisions
    - Notification streams: \c subscriptions/listen with a modern server, the standalone SSE
      event stream with an earlier-revision server
    - Tools: list and call server tools, with \c x-mcp-header parameter mirroring and
      validation
    - Resources: list and read server resources
    - Prompts: list and get server prompts with arguments
    - Completion: autocompletion for prompts and resources
    - Multi Round-Trip Requests: elicitation, sampling, and root-list requests are answered
      from callbacks and the original request is retried automatically
    - Tasks: a server-returned task handle is polled to completion transparently
    - Client-side caching of list results honoring their \c ttlMs and \c cacheScope hints
    - Logging: receive server log messages
    - Event callbacks for server notifications
    - Session management with Mcp-Session-Id header (earlier protocol revisions only)

    @subsection McpClient_usage Basic Usage

    @code{.py}
    %requires McpClient

    # Create client — the URL may include a sub-path
    McpClient client("https://server:8080/mcp");

    # Initialize connection
    hash<auto> server_info = client.initialize({
        "name": "MyApp",
        "version": "1.0",
    });

    # List and call tools
    list<auto> tools = client.listTools();
    hash<auto> result = client.callTool("echo", {"message": "Hello"});

    # List and read resources
    list<auto> resources = client.listResources();
    hash<auto> content = client.readResource("file:///config.json");

    # List and get prompts
    list<auto> prompts = client.listPrompts();
    hash<auto> messages = client.getPrompt("greeting", {"name": "Alice"});

    # Close connection
    client.close();
    @endcode

    @par SSE Notification Listener Example
    @code{.py}
    McpClient client("https://server:8080/mcp");
    client.initialize({"name": "MyApp", "version": "1.0"});

    # Register callbacks for server notifications
    client.setCallback("onToolsChanged", sub () { printf("Tools changed!\n"); });
    client.setCallback("onLogMessage", sub (string level, auto data, *string logger) {
        printf("[%s] %s: %s\n", level, logger ?? "server", data);
    });

    # Start the SSE listener for server push events
    client.startSseListener();

    # ... do work ...

    client.stopSseListener();
    client.close();
    @endcode

    @section McpClient_relnotes McpClient Release Notes

    @subsection McpClient_1_0 McpClient v1.0
    - added the @ref mcp_task_support "task support constants" naming the values a tool can
      declare in \c execution.taskSupport
    - added support for the @ref mcp_apps "MCP Apps extension" (\c io.modelcontextprotocol/ui) under the
      \c enable_ui option: the client negotiates the extension, surfaces \c _meta.ui through
      @ref McpClient::McpClient::getToolUiMeta() "getToolUiMeta()", and fetches an interface with its sandbox
      policy through @ref McpClient::McpClient::getUiResource() "getUiResource()".  Rendering is a browser's job
      and is deliberately out of scope
    - initial release with full MCP protocol support
    - async I/O via @ref HttpClientIo::HttpClientConnectionManager with automatic
      protocol negotiation and connection pooling
    - streamable HTTP transport with legacy HTTP fallback
    - SSE notification listener via @ref HttpClientStreamingIo::SseStreamReader
    - tools, resources, prompts, completion support
    - event callbacks for notifications
    - MCP 2026-07-28 support with transparent era detection (see @ref McpClient_eras):
      - per-request protocol metadata and \c MCP-Protocol-Version / \c Mcp-Method /
        \c Mcp-Name / \c Mcp-Param-* header mirroring, including the Base64 sentinel encoding
        for values that are not header-safe
      - @ref McpClient::McpClient::discover() "discover()" for \c server/discover, and
        automatic renegotiation on
        @ref McpClient::MCP_Err_UnsupportedProtocolVersion "UnsupportedProtocolVersionError"
      - Multi Round-Trip Requests: \c onElicitation, \c onSampling, and \c onListRoots
        callbacks answer the server's input requests and the original request is retried
        automatically with the server's opaque state echoed back unmodified
      - @ref McpClient::McpClient::listen() "listen()" opens a \c subscriptions/listen stream
        and demultiplexes notifications by subscription ID
      - the \c io.modelcontextprotocol/tasks extension: a task handle returned by the server
        is polled to completion by
        @ref McpClient::McpClient::callTool() "callTool()", including mid-flight
        \c tasks/update input
      - tools whose \c x-mcp-header annotations violate the specification are excluded from
        @ref McpClient::McpClient::listTools() "listTools()" with a logged warning, rather
        than failing the whole listing
      - list results are cached according to their \c ttlMs and \c cacheScope hints
    - server-to-client requests (sampling, elicitation, roots) are answered from callbacks on
      handshake-based protocol revisions too; previously such requests were received on the
      event stream and silently discarded, so a server that asked for input waited until it
      timed out
    - the deprecated HTTP+SSE transport of protocol 2024-11-05 is supported: when a server
      rejects a Streamable HTTP POST, the client opens the event stream, takes the endpoint
      from the \c endpoint event, and correlates each response that arrives on the stream with
      the request that is waiting for it
    - registering an \c onElicitation, \c onSampling, or \c onListRoots callback declares the
      corresponding client capability, and removing it withdraws the capability
*/

#! the %McpClient namespace holds all public definitions in the %McpClient module
public namespace McpClient {
#! Client name
public const McpClientName = "QoreMcpClient";

#! Client version
public const McpClientVersion = "1.0";
}
